<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Georg Marcgraf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1610">1610</a>–<a href="/years/1644">1644</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Georg Marcgrave</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Georg Marcgraf was a German naturalist, astronomer and cartographer who joined Johan Maurits in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, where he built an observatory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a> and studied the plants, animals and geography of the colony. His work formed the core of the Historia Naturalis Brasiliae, published with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willem-piso">Willem Piso</a> in 1648, the first major scientific study of Brazil. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 1644.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georg-marcgraf.jpg" alt="Georg Marcgraf" width="320" height="506" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Historia-Naturalis-Brasiliae.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
